#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# radon_transform

#% text
# `radon_transform(f, [x, y], [p, phi])` gives the Radon transform of f: the integral of f over the line `x cos(phi) + y sin(phi) = p`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The line is parametrized by `x = p cos(phi) - t sin(phi)`, `y = p sin(phi) + t cos(phi)` and the integral over the real t evaluated in closed form; the result is simplified with `trig_reduce`.

#% item
# Numbers for p and phi give the value on that line.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
radon_transform(exp(-x^2 - y^2), [x, y], [p, phi])
#% output
#= exp(-p^2)*pi^(1/2)

#% cell
radon_transform(x*exp(-x^2 - y^2), [x, y], [p, phi])
#% output
#= p*cos(phi)*exp(-p^2)*pi^(1/2)

#% cell
radon_transform(x*exp(-x^2 - y^2), [x, y], [3, pi/3])
#% output
#= 3*pi^(1/2)/(2*exp(9))

#% cell
radon_transform((x^2 + 5*y^2)*exp(-x^2 - y^2), [x, y], [p, phi])
#% output
#= (1/2)*(3 + 2*cos(2*phi) + 6*p^2 - 4*cos(2*phi)*p^2)*exp(-p^2)*pi^(1/2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `integrate`, `fourier_transform`, `hankel_transform`
